{% extends 'layouts/base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}SWAT - Documentation - Overview{% endblock %}

{% block body %}

    <main>

        <section class="documentation-overview-page">

            <div class="hero">
                <h1 class="hero_title">Overview</h1>
                <p class="hero_subtitle">
                    Understand the philosophy behind SWAT and how the different components work together.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">What is SWAT</span>
            </div>

            <div class="overview-intro">

                <p class="overview-intro_text">
                    SWAT is a lightweight Symfony toolkit designed to simplify modern development and deployment workflows.
                    It combines a clean Symfony application structure, a minimal frontend stack, and a ready-to-use
                    GitHub Actions pipeline for automated deployments.
                </p>

                <p class="overview-intro_text">
                    The goal of SWAT is not to provide a rigid framework, but a practical and production-ready starting point
                    that developers can understand, use and adapt to their own needs.
                </p>

            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">How SWAT works</span>
            </div>

            <div class="overview-flow">

                <div class="overview-flow_step">
                    <div class="overview-flow_title">1. Develop</div>
                    <div class="overview-flow_description">
                        Work on your Symfony application with a lightweight frontend stack based on Twig, SCSS and TypeScript.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="overview-flow_step">
                    <div class="overview-flow_title">2. Validate</div>
                    <div class="overview-flow_description">
                        GitHub Actions runs the pipeline to build, validate and prepare the project for deployment.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="overview-flow_step">
                    <div class="overview-flow_title">3. Deploy</div>
                    <div class="overview-flow_description">
                        SWAT deploys the application over SSH using environment-specific secrets and server configuration.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="overview-flow_step">
                    <div class="overview-flow_title">4. Run</div>
                    <div class="overview-flow_description">
                        The target server runs the Symfony application with its own environment variables and deployment settings.
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">Main components</span>
            </div>

            <div class="overview-components">

                <article class="overview-components_card">
                    <div class="overview-components_icon">⚙️</div>
                    <h2 class="overview-components_title">Symfony application</h2>
                    <p class="overview-components_description">
                        The backend handles routing, controllers, templates and application logic using a clean Symfony structure.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article class="overview-components_card">
                    <div class="overview-components_icon">🎨</div>
                    <h2 class="overview-components_title">Frontend stack</h2>
                    <p class="overview-components_description">
                        Twig, SCSS and TypeScript provide a modern frontend foundation without adding unnecessary complexity.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article class="overview-components_card">
                    <div class="overview-components_icon">🚀</div>
                    <h2 class="overview-components_title">GitHub Actions pipeline</h2>
                    <p class="overview-components_description">
                        The CI/CD workflow builds, validates and deploys the project automatically through GitHub Actions.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article class="overview-components_card">
                    <div class="overview-components_icon">🖥️</div>
                    <h2 class="overview-components_title">Server environment</h2>
                    <p class="overview-components_description">
                        Environment variables, SSH access and optional reverse proxy support complete the deployment setup.
                    </p>
                </article>

            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">Design philosophy</span>
            </div>

            <div class="overview-principles">

                <article class="overview-principles_item">
                    <h2 class="overview-principles_title">Minimal by design</h2>
                    <p class="overview-principles_description">
                        SWAT avoids unnecessary tooling and focuses on a simple, understandable stack.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article class="overview-principles_item">
                    <h2 class="overview-principles_title">Developer-first</h2>
                    <p class="overview-principles_description">
                        The project is designed to stay readable, practical and easy to adapt.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article class="overview-principles_item">
                    <h2 class="overview-principles_title">Production-ready</h2>
                    <p class="overview-principles_description">
                        SWAT is built to support real deployment workflows, not just local experimentation.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article class="overview-principles_item">
                    <h2 class="overview-principles_title">Flexible</h2>
                    <p class="overview-principles_description">
                        The provided structure is a solid base, but it can be customized to fit your own workflow.
                    </p>
                </article>

            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">Next steps</span>
            </div>

            <div class="next-steps">

                <p class="next-steps_text">
                    Ready to go deeper? Continue with the technical documentation to explore the CI/CD pipeline,
                    the project structure and the available customization options.
                </p>

                <div class="next-steps_actions">

                    <a href="{{ path('app_documentation_ci_cd') }}" class="next-steps_button">
                        CI/CD Pipeline
                    </a>

                    <a href="{{ path('app_documentation_project_structure') }}" class="next-steps_button">
                        Project Structure
                    </a>

                    <a href="{{ path('app_documentation_customization') }}" class="next-steps_button">
                        Customization
                    </a>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>

    </main>

{% endblock %}